  "answer": "A logo can help distinguish marks, but its presence is not a guarantee against deceptive similarity; equally, the absence of a logo is not an automatic finding of deceptive similarity. This is the practical lesson of the contrasting examples supplied in the Manual. [TMM/Part26/6/6/8~3; TMM/Part26/6/6/8~4]\n\n**Act — governing test:** Section 10 asks whether one trade mark so nearly resembles another that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion. The statutory test is resemblance and its likely effect, not simply whether a logo is present. [TMA1995/s10]\n\n**Manual practice — how the examples illustrate that test:**\n- In *Combined Communications*, the Manual reproduces a finding that the plain-word mark lacked an accompanying device to help purchasers distinguish it. The word mark was found deceptively similar, whereas the logo mark was not. A device can therefore make a meaningful distinguishing contribution. [TMM/Part26/6/6/8~3]\n- In the *realcommercial* example, differences in font, colour, the house device and additional features were insufficient to overcome the danger created by the common essential feature. A logo may therefore fail to distinguish marks where that shared feature remains decisive. [TMM/Part26/6/6/8~4]\n\nFor examination, the Manual requires a real tangible danger, rather than a mere possibility, of deception or confusion. An ordinary person's reasonable doubt can suffice, and all surrounding circumstances must be considered. The logo's contribution should be assessed within that enquiry, rather than treated as a categorical rule. [TMM/Part26/2#1]\n\n**Separate Act ground:** Section 43 concerns confusion arising from a connotation of the mark or a sign within it, rather than the section 10 comparison with another mark. [TMA1995/s43; TMA1995/s10]"
